gfx803 (Polaris, GCN3): emit the packed v_dot intrinsics only where the
instruction exists.

Applies to: triton-lang/triton 0a6e13e6e31f18fa75a17ac72dee0dd1bcb507b8
(release/3.9.x). Apply it after gfx803-isa-family.patch: its TargetFeatures.cpp
hunk keeps that patch's `case ISAFamily::GCN3:` line as context.

WHY
---
On the FMA path, triton lowers two dot shapes to packed intrinsics on every
target. An fp16 dot with f32 accumulation becomes `llvm.amdgcn.fdot2`. An i8 dot
with i32 accumulation becomes `llvm.amdgcn.sdot4`. gfx803 has only the FMA path
(no MFMA, no WMMA), and it has neither instruction. In LLVM's AMDGPU backend,
fdot2 needs dot10-insts and sdot4 needs dot1-insts or dot8-insts, and gfx803's
feature set has none of them. LLVM finds no instruction to select and aborts
codegen instead of falling back:

    LLVM ERROR: Cannot select: t1002: f32 = AMDGPUISD::FDOT2
    In function: _fwd_kernel

A 16x16 fp16 `tl.dot` reproduces it on its own. No vLLM, MIGraphX or ROCm stack
patch is involved:

    @triton.jit
    def dot_kernel(a, b, c, BM: tl.constexpr, BN: tl.constexpr, BK: tl.constexpr):
        rm, rn, rk = tl.arange(0, BM), tl.arange(0, BN), tl.arange(0, BK)
        A = tl.load(a + rm[:, None] * BK + rk[None, :])
        B = tl.load(b + rk[:, None] * BN + rn[None, :])
        tl.store(c + rm[:, None] * BN + rn[None, :], tl.dot(A, B))

fp32 dots use the `fmuladd` cells and are unaffected. bf16 dots are unaffected
too, because `tryLegalizeFMA` converts them to fp32 before this lowering runs.
The fp16 case decides whether anything runs at all: every attention and prefill
kernel of vLLM's ROCm attention backend is an fp16 dot. A triton that cannot
compile one cannot start a vLLM engine. The engine dies in core init with "Engine
core initialization failed", and the LLVM abort above is the only explanation it
prints.

Upstream 2defcb725 ("[AMD] Create TargetFeatures for architectural checks",
triton-lang/triton#10142) moved the arch predicates into the `TargetFeatures`
class. It dropped the v_dot check at all three of its call sites, and
`TargetFeatures` has no v_dot predicate to put back:

  - `AMDFMAVectorMultiplier::chooseIntrinsic` (FMA.cpp) builds the fdot2/sdot4
    cells unconditionally.
  - `isLegalFMAForm` (AccelerateAMDMatmul.cpp) reports the fp16 -> f32 and
    i8 -> i32 forms legal on every target. The dot keeps that form all the way
    into the FMA lowering, and nothing converts it to one common type.
  - `tryAccelerateF16WithVDot` lost its guard as well. It is unreachable today,
    because its caller tests it under `if (false && ...)`, but it makes the same
    assumption and breaks the same way once that TODO is enabled.

WHAT
----
1. `TargetFeatures.h`/`TargetFeatures.cpp`: add `supportsVDot()`. It is false
   for `GCN3`, and for `Unknown`, which the backend rejects anyway. It is true
   for every other family, so the lowering on every other target stays exactly
   as upstream has it.
2. `DotOpToLLVM/FMA.cpp`: if `supportsVDot()` is false, do not build the
   fdot2/sdot4 cells.
3. `AccelerateAMDMatmul.cpp`: gate the three v_dot forms in `isLegalFMAForm` on
   `supportsVDot()`, so a target without v_dot falls through to the uniform-type
   test at the end of that function, and guard `tryAccelerateF16WithVDot`.

WHAT THIS COSTS ON GFX803
-------------------------
Without v_dot, `isLegalFMAForm` rejects the mixed-type forms. `tryLegalizeFMA`
then converts the whole dot to one common type that the plain FMA cells can
lower. That type is f32 for both affected forms.

- fp16 x fp16 -> f32: the operands convert exactly. The result matches what the
  packed path computes. The cost is the bandwidth that packing saves.
- i8 x i8 -> i32: the operands convert exactly, but the i32 accumulator goes
  through `sitofp`, accumulates in f32 and comes back through `fptosi`. f32
  holds every integer exactly only up to 2^24. An i8 dot whose accumulator, or
  any partial sum inside one dot, exceeds 2^24 in magnitude therefore rounds,
  silently. Each i8 x i8 product is at most 2^14 in magnitude. So one dot with K
  above 1024 can get there with worst-case inputs. A loop-carried accumulator
  gets there after enough K steps. This is the rounding that
  upstream 9c12f2f11 ("[AMD] Preserve i32 accumulation for small-K int8 dots")
  names for the f32 route. That commit's own fix keeps i8 dots on sdot4, which is
  the instruction gfx803 lacks. An exact i8 dot on gfx803 needs an integer
  multiply-add cell in the FMA lowering, which it does not have: it asserts on
  integer element types.

Before this patch, every fp16 and i8 dot on gfx803 aborts the compile. With it,
fp16 dots are exact and i8 dots are exact only while the accumulator stays
within 2^24.

VERIFICATION
------------
NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE on triton 3.9. The last hardware run used
triton 3.8 (675c59878) on an RX 570: a 16x16 `tl.dot` per dtype, compiled and
run, GPU result against a float64 CPU reference.

| dtype | without the patch  | with the patch                                         |
| ----- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| fp16  | LLVM abort (FDOT2) | OK, max_err 1.19e-06, no fdot2 in the LLIR, asm is `v_mul_f32`/`v_add_f32` |
| i8    | LLVM abort (sdot4) | OK, max_err 0.0, no sdot4 in the LLIR                  |
| fp32  | OK                 | OK, max_err 1.69e-06                                   |
| bf16  | OK                 | OK, max_err 4.77e-07                                   |

With that build, `vllm 0.20.1+gfx803` started an engine on Qwen3-0.6B and
generated coherent text (greedy, 64 tokens, 46.6 tok/s).

Repro to run on this commit, on gfx803:
1. The 16x16 `tl.dot` above for fp16, i8, fp32 and bf16, each against a float64
   CPU reference, and a grep of the LLIR for fdot2 and sdot4, which must find
   neither.
2. An i8 dot with K = 2048 and every input at 127. The exact result is
   2048 * 16129 = 33032192. Past 2^24, every second partial sum is odd, and f32
   cannot hold an odd integer there. The result shows whether the accumulator
   rounds as described above, and by how much.
3. A vLLM engine start and a short greedy generation on Qwen3-0.6B.

